-- title:
--   Units of A[1/π] are A^× · π^ℤ
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative ring which is a Noetherian integral domain, and let $K$ be a field equipped with an $A$-algebra structure making it a fraction field of $A$ (so that `algebraMap A K` is the canonical injection). Let $\pi \in A$ satisfy that the principal ideal $(\pi)$ generated by $\pi$ is prime, and that $\pi \neq 0$. Let $a \in K$ be nonzero, and assume both of the following: there exist a natural number $m$ and an element $b \in A$ with $a \cdot \pi^{m} = b$ in $K$, and there exist a natural number $m'$ and an element $c \in A$ with $a^{-1} \cdot \pi^{m'} = c$ in $K$ (powers and images taken via `algebraMap A K`); that is, both $a$ and $a^{-1}$ lie in the subring $A[1/\pi]$ of $K$. The conclusion asserts the existence of an integer $n$ and a unit $u \in A^{\times}$ such that $a = \iota(u) \cdot \iota(\pi)^{n}$ in $K$, where $\iota =$ `algebraMap A K` and the power is an integer power of the nonzero element $\iota(\pi)$.
--
--   This is the elementary commutative-algebra ingredient behind the descent step in the construction of Néron models (Bosch–Lütkebohmert–Raynaud, 4.3, Lemma 1): the units of the localisation $A[1/\pi]$ are exactly $A^{\times}$ times integer powers of $\pi$. It is used here in the treatment of top-degree differential forms, where it yields a comparison of two bases up to a unit times a power of the uniformiser.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_IsNoetherianRing_exists_unit_mul_zpow_eq_of_span_singleton_isPrime.lean

